name=bitewrite
version=4.3
desc=A page where every letter lands like the launcher's — write, notes and code in folders, line numbers, diffs, a tabbed little browser, lyrics that play, typing speed
category=visual
author=GLITCH-BITE-404
exec=run
deps=qml6:qt6-declarative python3:python py:numpy:python-numpy
usage=bitewrite   ·   opens a window · F1 write · F2 notes · F3 code · F4 lyrics · F5 speed · F6 settings
nopreview=bitewrite opens a window — there is nothing to sample into a text pane. Press Enter on it in INSTALLED to open the real thing.
keys=F1:WRITE — free writing, kept for next time|F2:NOTES — as many notes as you like, saved as you type|F3:CODE — your files: JavaScript, HTML, Python or Bash, run with Ctrl+Enter|F4:LYRICS — type a song and it plays with you|F5:SPEED — a typing test, wpm live at the bottom|F6:settings — animations, sounds, direction, everything|Ctrl+Enter:run what you wrote / show your HTML in the little browser / play-pause the song|Esc or ■ stop:CODE — stop a running program (keys and mouse go to it)|Esc:CODE — put the little browser away · your tabs are still there next time|Ctrl+T Ctrl+W:in the little browser — another tab · close this one|Ctrl+Tab Alt+1…9:in the little browser — switch pages|Alt+← Alt+→:in the little browser — back and forward|Ctrl+R Ctrl+= Ctrl+-:in the little browser — reload, zoom in, zoom out|Ctrl+F:CODE — finish the program and edit it|Ctrl+N:a new note · a new CODE file · another song or text|Ctrl+K:NOTES or CODE — find a note or a file|Alt+↑↓:NOTES or CODE — the note or file above / below|Ctrl+Shift+Del:NOTES or CODE — delete this note or file (press twice)|Ctrl+E:CODE — rename this file (a / puts it in a folder, its ending picks the language) · NOTES — move this note to a folder|Ctrl+Shift+N:NOTES or CODE — a new folder|Ctrl+D:CODE — what changed: this file against the last time you ran it, or against another file|Ctrl+I:NOTES or CODE — install this one into Documents/bitewrite (a .py or .sh also becomes a command)|Ctrl+Shift+I:install everything you've made, notes and code, folders and all|Tab / Shift+Tab:SPEED — change the time / the text|Ctrl+R:start this one over|Ctrl+H:hide the ghost|Ctrl+S:save a copy to Documents|Ctrl+L:clear|Ctrl+= Ctrl+-:bigger / smaller|Ctrl+M:mute|Esc:back to WRITE, or close
about=The launcher's search box is the most satisfying thing in the shell to type into, and it only ever holds one short line. This is that box turned into a whole page, and then into five things to do with it. It writes Hebrew and Arabic right to left — an English word inside a Hebrew line still reads correctly, brackets face the right way — and Chinese, Japanese and emoji take two cells. Every letter is its own animated object. By default it uses the launcher's exact pop (grows from a third of its size with the same OutBack overshoot, rises ten pixels, fades in over 140ms), and the settings swap that for drop, glitch, stamp, spin, float or random. Backspace can shrink letters away, drop them off the page, burst them into dust, flick them sideways or tear them apart. There are nine sound packs. launcher is the shell's own click at your sfx volume. The other eight are synthesised on first run: typewriter, thock, bubble, glitch, rain, and musicbox and chiptune, where every letter is a fixed note so your words come out as tunes. NOTES keeps as many notes as you like, each saved as you type, titled by its first line, with a list beside the page. CODE is a folder, not a page: as many files as you like down the side, each with a name you chose, saved as you type, with line numbers beside the page, and Alt+↑↓ or a click moves between them. A / in a name makes a folder (site/index.html), + folder or Ctrl+Shift+N makes one, and a click on a folder folds it. NOTES has folders the same way — Ctrl+E moves a note into one. Ctrl+D shows what changed, the way any editor does: this file against the last version you ran, or against another file, line by line with + and −, and Tab jumps from change to change. Ctrl+I installs what you made — into Documents/bitewrite, folders kept, and a Python or Bash file also lands in ~/.local/bin as a command named after it — and Ctrl+Shift+I installs everything, notes included. A file's ending is its language — rename page.js to page.html and Ctrl+Enter stops running it and starts drawing it. It takes JavaScript, which runs inside the window, HTML, which is drawn inside the window too, or Python and Bash, which run in your terminal, and runs whatever you wrote with Ctrl+Enter: broken code doesn't run and says so, valid code runs full-screen. You can start from a blank page (Enter indents after a brace, a closing brace steps back out) or type one of 32 programs over its faint ghost — from easy to extreme: a rainbow snake, a spinning cube, boids, metaballs, a mandelbrot dive, the spinning ASCII donut, doom fire — and press Ctrl+F to finish it and go straight to changing it. Programs can be played: while one runs, the keyboard and the mouse belong to it (keys, mouse, onKey, onClick), and only Esc or the stop button bring you back — so there are games too: snake, pong, flappy dot, a swarm that chases your mouse, and a paint program. HTML gets a browser that isn't one, and six pages to type over a ghost, from a first heading-and-paragraph to a whole styled article: no browser is started, nothing is opened, no network is touched — the tabs, the address bar, the reload sweep and the status line are drawn here, and each page is Qt's rich-text engine, which reads a real subset of HTML and CSS. Headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, links, quotes, colours, fonts and page backgrounds all land; scripts, canvas, video, forms, flexbox and grid do not, and instead of quietly dropping them the status line counts them out loud. It holds as many pages open at once as you like: every Ctrl+Enter sends what you just wrote to a new tab, so the last dozen things you tried are all still there to flip between, and Esc only puts the browser away — the tabs are waiting when you run the next one. Ctrl+T opens the start page, which lists every page this machine has — your own files first, the samples under them — and a link from one page to another really does go there, by the name you gave it, with its own history per tab, so ‹ and › mean something. Two files that link to each other browse like a small site, and every visit, reload, back and forward asks the file again rather than showing the copy it kept, so a page you changed in the editor is the page you see here. A link to the outside world lands on an offline page and a link to a file that isn't here lands on a not-found page, both written on the spot. Ctrl+W closes a tab, Ctrl+Tab switches, Alt+← goes back, Ctrl+= and Ctrl+- zoom. A page that doesn't close its tags doesn't draw, and the header says which line opened the tag that is still open. Long lines scroll the page sideways instead of running off the edge. LYRICS takes whatever is playing in Spotify, YouTube or mpv, or a file from your Music folder, finds its lyrics and makes them the ghost. In follow flow the song plays through the line you're on and waits at the start of the next one until you get there. Finishing a line and typing on drops you onto the next one. SPEED is a typing test. Pick 15, 30, 60 or 120 seconds, infinite, or a custom time, and random words, sentences, hard text or your own page. Your speed is live at the bottom the whole time, with accuracy, the clock and a sparkline. A timed run ends on a results card that keeps your best for each combination. In LYRICS and SPEED every key fills the next letter's slot and the line never moves: a wrong letter stays red where it is and you carry on, extra letters are refused, and a space mid-word skips to the next word. In CODE, mistakes are counted word by word, so one slip costs one word. Every setting is in the F6 panel: it applies live while you type, the wheel scrolls it, Delete resets one setting, and a row at the bottom resets them all.
